00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.992
ARPITA SHRIVASTAVA: All
right, so Cloud Datastore--

00:02:55.992 --> 00:02:58.200
the way we're going to be
talking about the databases

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.350
is just give you a little
bit of an introduction

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:05.160
why we chose that database
service, our takeaways

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:10.830
from using that service, and
how our data needs evolved

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:14.495
to want a newer solution.

00:03:14.495 --> 00:03:17.550
So Datastore-- it's a
NoSQL document store.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:21.510
Your data is organized into
documents called entities,

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:23.970
which are basically
objects that are accessed

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:28.320
via a key or an index value.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.340
And similar entities
are grouped as kinds,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.190
which is like a table
in a relational model.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:42.780
Entities have fields called
properties, which are basically

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:43.680
the values--

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.980
you get two inbuilt indexes
on each of the properties

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.900
for an entity.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:53.300
There are no joins or aggregate
queries for Datastore.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.690
There is the GQL, which is a
query language for Datastore,

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:03.160
but complex joints and aggregate
operations are not supported--

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.440
but simple filtering
is possible.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:08.560
Why we chose it?

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.810
So back when Streak was started,
it was started on App Engine.

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:16.800
So the only database
offering at the time was--

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.500
on Google Cloud-- was
Datastore, so it made sense

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.040
to collocate our database
with our server stack

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.230
on Google Cloud.

00:04:25.230 --> 00:04:27.840
But even so, we think
it's an excellent choice

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.270
for if you're starting out
with a new product or service,

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.790
and that is because it gives
you a lot of flexibility

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:38.190
to evolve your schema.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.140
And what that means
with Datastore

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:45.150
is, with entities, as I said,
they have different properties.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.450
You can have-- the same
properties across entities

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.730
can be completely different.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:52.470
The number of properties,
even the data types

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:54.560
for the same properties,
can be different,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.740
so it gives you a lot of freedom
to just build your features

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:02.430
and evolve your
schema as you go.

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.750
Of course, the other reason
we love Datastore is it's

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.040
a managed service.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:08.580
NoOps.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.520
It autoscales, autoreplicates.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.600
Google's SREs
handle availability.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:19.530
You don't have to worry about
provisioning capacity up front,

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:22.890
and you really just
throw data at it,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.930
and it would just grow
with your service.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.850
And so back then, there
was one candidate,

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:33.780
and over 10 years later,
we have about 15 terabytes

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:34.740
of data on Datastore.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.790
All our application
data is on it,

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:42.630
and we found it to perform
predictably and have

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:46.121
predictable kind of
challenges and solutions.

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:51.220
So working with Datastore.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.990
So some concepts
that we found are

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:57.010
key to work most
effectively with Datastore.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:59.290
One is entity groups.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:03.730
So as I was saying, your data
is organized into entities.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:09.640
And multiple entities, if they
belong to one entity group--

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:13.630
you can have a transaction
across multiple entities.

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:18.070
So you, basically, want to have
your most highly correlated

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.340
data in one entity group,
so it can be a user profile,

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:23.160
and so on.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.850
At Streak, we have the
smallest unit of work is a box.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:30.220
So a box can have
different threads

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:34.600
of emails, notes, comments, and
a lot of other custom fields.

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:40.070
And so boxes can be
put into pipelines,

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.610
which are shared across
teams, and the teams

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:44.660
belong to an organization.

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:47.950
So the way you design
your entity groups

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.460
will have consequences on
throughput and consistency.

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:57.610
So for our case, if you
want to update boxes,

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.420
we have our entity group set
to organization level, which

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:05.380
means we have a lower
number of entity groups.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.880
What that does is--

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:11.180
so entity groups are also
units of consistency,

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.350
which means a query
against a entity group

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:18.730
will always give you
strongly consistent results.

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:23.140
Also, with a transaction,
they will always--

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.820
your asset
transaction properties

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.090
will be applied when
you're using entity groups.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.980
They're also stored--
all entities--

00:07:29.980 --> 00:07:31.360
within an entity group.

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:34.660
Google stores them
physically close together,

00:07:34.660 --> 00:07:36.900
so you have minimal IoT.

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:39.130
Reads are really fast.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:43.540
So in our example with
boxes, when the entity group

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.840
is set to an organization
level, with one

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:49.450
query against an
entity group, you

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:52.400
get a high volume of entities.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.400
But there are limits on how many
entity groups, how many writes

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:01.690
you can have per entity group
per second, which is set to you

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.330
can only have one write,
currently, in Datastore

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.130
per second per entity group.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.350
And I'll talk about
the consequences

00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:11.750
of that in a second.

00:08:11.750 --> 00:08:15.340
The other things that we found
useful working with Datastore

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:22.060
is we use Objectify, which is a
Java data access API that makes

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.890
using Datastore really easy.

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:28.510
The example at the bottom,
where you have annotations

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:31.510
to work with entities,
indexes, and so on,

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:32.900
has been really useful.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.350
We use JSON and
Jackson annotations

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.044
for serialization
and digitalization.

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:44.310
Then some of the challenges.

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.040
As I was saying, the
write throughput rate

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:51.600
for entity groups is, in
Datastore, set to 1 per second.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:55.590
So ideally, you'd want to have
really small entity groups

00:08:55.590 --> 00:09:01.680
so that you are not writing
more than once per second.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:05.220
In our case,
organizations-- pipelines

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:07.180
can have hundreds and
thousands of boxes.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:09.780
So for example, if
you're trying to update

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:12.930
multiple boxes, in our
case, in a pipeline,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:16.710
then it can be tens of thousands
of writes against one entity

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.580
group, so it can cause
a lot of contention.

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:22.590
And again, something to
keep in mind, entity group

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:24.840
relationships are permanent.

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.580
You cannot change them on this.

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:28.930
You delete all the
entities and recreate them

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:31.630
in a new entity group.

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:33.630
So that is a
challenge contention.

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:36.210
As we're growing, we have
larger and larger customers.

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:40.520
That has been a big challenge.

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:44.810
Again, boxes--
the way our schema

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.930
has evolved, there's
a lot of child objects

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:51.980
that we have with
boxes now that they all

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:55.130
belong to the same entity
group, but transactions

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:57.990
are limited to 500
entities per transaction.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:09:59.690
So when you have
cascading writes,

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:05.060
that also becomes kind of
a performance bottleneck.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.080
Also, another limit
to keep in mind--

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:11.300
transactions are limited,
only, to 25 entity groups.

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.450
So again, you want to
figure out your key design,

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.160
so that you're finding
the right balance

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:21.840
between your consistency
and throughput.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.870
And then client-side views.

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.170
So as you saw in
the first graphic,

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:30.530
we present this spreadsheet
layout to our users,

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:36.650
and we offer complex filtering,
grouping, and sorting.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.550
And not being able to do that--

00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:42.920
Datastore not providing
that out of the box--

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:45.050
we have to shift
this responsibility

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:48.320
to the application code,
which, of course, increases

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.770
the complexity and debugging
complexity and so on.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:55.760
So not having joints--

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:01.490
as we grow larger, we really
miss having all those features

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.710
supported out of the
box, which would be

00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:05.870
part of a relational database.

00:11:09.550 --> 00:11:11.800
Cloud Firestore.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:16.700
Firestore is the latest
version of Datastore.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.110
There's two modes here--

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.240
Realtime and Datastore mode.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:23.360
Datastore mode-- Firestore
in Datastore mode

00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:26.780
is backwards compatible
with Cloud Datastore,

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:28.850
but it doesn't have
some of the newer

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:34.200
features that Realtime Firestore
has, like real-time updates.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.730
There's rich mobiling
web client libraries.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:41.840
So Firestore is supposed
to be drop-in replacement

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:47.600
for Datastore, and the
migration to Firestore

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:51.120
is supposed to be automatic
without any downtime,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.020
so we're really
excited about that.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:57.350
Basically, Firestore
has a Spanner backend,

00:11:57.350 --> 00:11:59.600
with the Datastore
features in the front end.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.960
So you get a strongly
consistent layer in the backend,

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:05.940
and this eliminates some
of our biggest pain points

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:06.890
with Datastore.

00:12:06.890 --> 00:12:11.030
One, we have strongly
consistent results throughout,

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:13.970
and the entity
group restrictions

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:15.140
that I talked about.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:18.890
25 entity groups per transaction
and the write throughput limit

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.660
of 1 per second--

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.510
we don't have that
with Firestore anymore.

00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:27.774
We're hoping to
upgrade soon and--

00:12:27.774 --> 00:12:30.760
yeah.

00:12:30.760 --> 00:12:31.530
All right.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:36.060
So we talked about how we felt
the need for a fully relational

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.610
model for our application
data, and there's

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:45.030
a new feature that
we worked on, and we

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:46.620
call it the Metadata Problem.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:51.930
So I'm sure all of you had this
problem where somebody forgot

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.410
to hit Reply All in
an email, and then you

00:12:55.410 --> 00:12:58.230
got a forward of that email.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:01.830
And then now you have a lot
of redundant emails and emails

00:13:01.830 --> 00:13:03.750
just in reverse
chronological order,

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:08.400
and it's just not very elegant.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:11.880
And also, there can be problems
with permissions and sensitive

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:14.760
email that you were not
supposed to receive ends up

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.450
in your inbox.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:21.180
So strict solution to that
is having a unified view

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:25.020
of emails across a team.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:28.260
So in this example, you
have the top three emails

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:31.440
were not addressed to
me but, as you can see,

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:34.260
they're shared from my
co-worker's inboxes.

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.810
So you get kind of
this elegant view

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:39.600
of the email, which is
controlled by a permission

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:40.680
model.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:44.350
So this looks really
good in the UI.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:48.990
But on the backend, it's kind
of a complex graph traversal

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.920
problem.

00:13:50.920 --> 00:13:57.390
So we want to answer questions
like, which co-workers of mine

00:13:57.390 --> 00:14:03.870
have interacted with this
particular prospect over email,

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:08.010
or, for this email, what
are the other emails

00:14:08.010 --> 00:14:09.750
on this thread that
are not in my inbox

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.790
but in my co-workers' inboxes?

00:14:11.790 --> 00:14:14.370
So the solution to that--

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:17.490
we came up with
a graph solution,

00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:21.900
where your nodes are either
a person or an email,

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:27.060
an organization or a
domain, or an email message.

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:30.840
And the email
messages are connected

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:33.190
if they're on the same thread.

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:36.960
So if I emailed you, and
then you forwarded the email

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:39.750
to somebody else, we
can trace those emails

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:41.740
and give you a unified view.

00:14:41.740 --> 00:14:43.620
And then two emails
are also connected

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:47.820
if they are the same
email, but they're the part

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:49.950
of different people's inboxes.

00:14:49.950 --> 00:14:52.020
They're connected
by something that is

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:54.930
called an RFC ID with Gmail--

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:57.930
so same message but
different inboxes.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:15:01.830
So this, obviously, helps
us to answer the questions

00:15:01.830 --> 00:15:07.380
and create a unified view that
can be shared across a team.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:11.580
To talk about how we approach
this problem solving, this,

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.440
and the relational model needs--

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:16.360
uh, Fred?

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:17.630
FRED WULFF: Yeah, OK.

00:15:17.630 --> 00:15:21.980
Real quick before I go on to our
solution, just want to-- yeah,

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.570
building on what
Arpita was saying,

00:15:23.570 --> 00:15:26.910
so this is like a pretty
challenging graph database

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:27.410
problem.

00:15:27.410 --> 00:15:29.730
Like you have to
really understand

00:15:29.730 --> 00:15:32.480
this course of an email
thread or a course

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.120
of like connected email threads
across different people's

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:37.140
inboxes, your organization.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.915
You might have to hit
hundreds of different inboxes.

00:15:39.915 --> 00:15:42.290
I'm sure you've all been on
these like horrible Reply All

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:44.660
threads that have
not just hundreds

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:50.030
of people in the company but,
like, thousands of emails when

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:54.970
you count all the email chains.

00:15:54.970 --> 00:15:57.950
And so, there's kind of
this graph database problem,

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:01.630
but it's also pretty challenging
for a couple of reasons,

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:02.930
technically.

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:07.030
So one is you have to be able to
do all of these edge traversals

00:16:07.030 --> 00:16:09.410
quickly.

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:11.390
There may be a lot
of emails or sort

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.330
of nodes from the graph problem.

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:17.510
And as Arpita
mentioned, permissions

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:18.620
are a huge deal, right?

00:16:18.620 --> 00:16:20.060
Email can be really sensitive.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:21.920
You can have a
password reset emails.

00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:25.340
You can have a manager
giving targeted feedback

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:29.953
to a report that might not
be generally consumable.

00:16:29.953 --> 00:16:31.370
There are just
like all these ways

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:33.440
they can go horribly
wrong if you don't have

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.370
a strong permissions model.

00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:40.250
And kind of what this means is
we have to traverse this graph,

00:16:40.250 --> 00:16:43.220
and there's pretty
big challenges

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:46.070
if we try and precompute
a lot of this stuff.

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:47.930
Normally, the
distribute system's way

00:16:47.930 --> 00:16:51.480
out is you just like normalize
a bunch of stuff in advance.

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:53.540
And in this case, we
both really needed

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:55.732
to compute it at read
time to make sure

00:16:55.732 --> 00:16:58.190
that we're getting permissions
right, even if somebody just

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.890
changed some permissions.

00:17:00.890 --> 00:17:03.050
And this is all happening
within the context

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:04.520
of viewing emails in Gmail.

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:06.260
And you know, the
whole premise of Gmail

00:17:06.260 --> 00:17:07.670
is that it's really fast.

00:17:07.670 --> 00:17:10.430
People expect their emails
to just like load quickly,

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:12.319
even if they're searching
their whole inbox.

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:13.790
And we're expanding
that to letting

00:17:13.790 --> 00:17:18.020
you search parts of not just
your inbox, but everybody

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:20.510
in your company's
inbox possibly.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:23.180
So challenging
technical problem.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:26.569
Looking at the backend,
this is actually

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:28.890
across our entire user base.

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:32.602
We have about 30 terabytes
of just email metadata.

00:17:32.602 --> 00:17:35.060
So that's like we're not looking
at the contents of email--

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:36.710
we don't really want to--

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.720
but just looking at like
who the email's from,

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.030
who it's to, the
sort of information

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.400
we need to compute these edges.

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:46.500
And so we evaluated a bunch of
different database products.

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.590
There's a lot of
graph-specific database

00:17:48.590 --> 00:17:50.610
products on the market.

00:17:50.610 --> 00:17:53.100
But we really-- all
those being equal,

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:55.160
we wanted something that
had this property where

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.300
it was managed by Google.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:59.300
We have a very small
operational team.

00:17:59.300 --> 00:18:02.480
We basically have, roughly,
three infrastructure engineers,

00:18:02.480 --> 00:18:05.210
depending on how you slice
different people's time.

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:07.730
And being able to offload
some of that like availability

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:12.170
and charting work to
Google SRE really seemed

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:13.880
like a good trade-off to us.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:16.340
So we looked at a bunch
of different options,

00:18:16.340 --> 00:18:17.993
and we ended up--

00:18:17.993 --> 00:18:19.910
this was our first use
case for Cloud Spanner.

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:22.730
So Cloud Spanner--

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:25.370
I think it's about a year old at
this point, maybe a little bit

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:26.730
more.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.090
But the key things
about it, looking

00:18:29.090 --> 00:18:31.580
at just the product
in general, it

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:35.195
is a globally consistent
relational database.

00:18:35.195 --> 00:18:36.320
And so what does that mean?

00:18:36.320 --> 00:18:40.940
It supports the SQL language
that you know and possibly

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:44.900
love or hate, depending on
what you've done with it.

00:18:44.900 --> 00:18:49.460
And it has what I affectionately
call the magic clock thing.

00:18:49.460 --> 00:18:55.520
And the magic clock is basically
for years and years and years,

00:18:55.520 --> 00:18:57.830
there was always this
introductory paragraph

00:18:57.830 --> 00:19:01.250
in every distributed systems
paper, which basically

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.700
said something like, we know
clocks are unreliable and go

00:19:04.700 --> 00:19:08.480
back in time and have jumps,
and the network's unreliable

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.962
and may have horrible latency.

00:19:10.962 --> 00:19:12.920
And everybody just sort
of took it as a premise

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:16.100
that you couldn't
rely on clocks,

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:19.180
and they were trying
to lead you astray.

00:19:19.180 --> 00:19:21.050
And so the Spanner--

00:19:21.050 --> 00:19:22.490
Whitepaper came
out a while back,

00:19:22.490 --> 00:19:24.200
and now the Cloud
Spanner product

00:19:24.200 --> 00:19:26.450
is built on this
idea of, hey, what

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:29.120
if instead of accepting
this as a premise,

00:19:29.120 --> 00:19:31.680
we just made the clocks
roughly reliable?

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:34.250
And there's a bunch
of effort, in terms

00:19:34.250 --> 00:19:36.417
of installing atomic clocks
in all the data centers.

00:19:36.417 --> 00:19:38.667
There's a bunch of effort,
in terms of like setting up

00:19:38.667 --> 00:19:39.375
the networking.

00:19:39.375 --> 00:19:41.000
And there's a bunch
of effort, in terms

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:44.660
of the statistics of like
setting error bars on timing.

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:49.460
But the outcome of it is that
Cloud Spanner says on the tin,

00:19:49.460 --> 00:19:51.020
and it's actually
true, like we're

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:55.700
using it in [? anger. ?]
It is globally consistent.

00:19:55.700 --> 00:20:00.020
So basically, this means
that going back to the stuff

00:20:00.020 --> 00:20:03.140
that Arpita chatted
about with Datastore,

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:06.380
you don't have to preselect
particular entity groups.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:08.300
You don't have to say,
these are the things

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.760
that I'd like to do
transactions on beforehand.

00:20:10.760 --> 00:20:13.520
You just-- when you go to
actually update entities,

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:16.280
it looks at what
entities you've queried.

00:20:16.280 --> 00:20:18.680
And you only have
transactional conflicts

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:24.500
if you are actually modifying
things that were also

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:26.060
queried by other queries.

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:27.710
So it's like just
a set of stuff that

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:31.310
are actual real
transaction conflicts

00:20:31.310 --> 00:20:35.420
So, you know, this is great.

00:20:35.420 --> 00:20:42.560
Globally consistent SQL, it
has really great transactions.

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.630
And, you know,
some of you who are

00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:47.670
sort of thinking this
through might say, but wait--

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:49.880
email itself isn't consistent.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:52.670
Why do you care
about consistency?

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:55.700
I might send you an email,
and, all the time, we

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:59.930
see emails taking 10 minutes,
30 minutes to get delivered.

00:20:59.930 --> 00:21:03.028
Why do we care about the
database being consistent?

00:21:03.028 --> 00:21:04.820
And the answer is it
goes back to that sort

00:21:04.820 --> 00:21:06.830
of operational ease.

00:21:06.830 --> 00:21:09.890
It means that when we
have our syncing logic,

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:12.530
we can sync the data.

00:21:12.530 --> 00:21:16.730
We can build these indexes
to power these graph queries.

00:21:16.730 --> 00:21:20.300
And we don't have to
worry that the sync gets

00:21:20.300 --> 00:21:21.680
partially committed.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.500
We can just say,
hey, all of this

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:26.820
should be committed
in the transaction.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:32.830
And it's either all
committed or not committed.

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:36.050
And this just means that
our indexing pipeline

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:40.370
that's processing a lot of
email was basically written by--

00:21:40.370 --> 00:21:44.870
it basically took one engineer
about half-time working

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:46.800
on it for a month or so.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:50.077
It was like, really, for the
scale of distributed system.

00:21:50.077 --> 00:21:52.160
When we're planning out
the work, we're like, hey,

00:21:52.160 --> 00:21:54.830
we probably need to
have at least two people

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:56.420
on this for a quarter.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.330
And just by relying on the
Spanner transactionality

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:01.680
to deal with a lot
of the edge cases,

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:06.620
we were able to just get it out
and get it in customers' hands

00:22:06.620 --> 00:22:09.450
much more quickly.

00:22:09.450 --> 00:22:11.900
And so building on
that operational ease,

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:14.600
there's a lot of other sort
of really nice features

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.460
in that area around Spanner.

00:22:16.460 --> 00:22:21.650
So in a previous life, I managed
a couple of on-premise SQL

00:22:21.650 --> 00:22:25.020
databases and, sort of, all of
those operational things that

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.500
just sort of filled
DBAs with terror--

00:22:27.500 --> 00:22:30.140
things like adding and
removing columns, adding

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:32.420
or removing indexes.

00:22:32.420 --> 00:22:35.570
Both, they're like
pretty easy to do.

00:22:35.570 --> 00:22:40.430
And also, there's
a pretty good sort

00:22:40.430 --> 00:22:45.320
of Task Planner that makes
sure that, even if you

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:46.970
add and remove a
bunch of columns,

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:50.480
it doesn't interfere with your
actual transactional workload.

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:54.020
So that's all pretty
nice things to have,

00:22:54.020 --> 00:22:56.990
even if our underlying
data isn't necessarily

00:22:56.990 --> 00:23:01.690
super dependent on the
strong consistency.

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:05.530
So let's-- you know, I've been
singing its praises generally.

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:08.330
Let's talk about how it
actually works in practice.

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:12.970
So this is in the Cloud
Console, the schema for one

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:16.010
of our tables in production.

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:18.850
And this table is what
we call MessageMailboxes.

00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:22.127
And so that's-- if you remember
the sort of graph nodes,

00:23:22.127 --> 00:23:24.460
there were some things that
are dependent on the message

00:23:24.460 --> 00:23:28.780
itself, which Gmail thread it's
in, which other messages have

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:32.590
the same sort of message ID
header to figure it out between

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:33.580
inboxes.

00:23:33.580 --> 00:23:37.570
But there's also stuff that's
dependent on who the message is

00:23:37.570 --> 00:23:41.190
addressed to or who it's
from or who's on the CC list.

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:46.040
And MessageMailboxes has one
row per person on a message.

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:49.690
So if I send a message
to you, the version

00:23:49.690 --> 00:23:52.660
of the message in my inbox
would have two MessageMailboxes

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:53.230
rows--

00:23:53.230 --> 00:23:55.990
one for me and one for you.

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:58.150
And key things here--

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:00.640
so you see at the top,
it says that this table

00:24:00.640 --> 00:24:03.213
is interleaved in messages.

00:24:03.213 --> 00:24:04.630
Interleaved tables
is this feature

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:08.530
in Cloud Spanner that
lets you sort of have

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:10.270
really great performance
for stuff that

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:12.290
should be co-located together.

00:24:12.290 --> 00:24:14.260
And so here, most of
the time that we're

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.480
querying who's on a
particular message,

00:24:17.480 --> 00:24:19.930
we also care about what
message they're on.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:23.560
And so here, we've interleaved
MessageMailboxes in messages.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:28.240
And that means that if I want
to do an index filter on all

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:32.170
the messages that correspond
to a particular email address--

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:33.790
that a particular
email address was

00:24:33.790 --> 00:24:36.130
part of-- so let's say
in the Streak product,

00:24:36.130 --> 00:24:39.400
we want to be able to show
all of your communication

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:43.600
with a particular sales lead
across the organization,

00:24:43.600 --> 00:24:45.820
I can query on mailbox
email and then do

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:49.330
a very quick join,
very performance join,

00:24:49.330 --> 00:24:51.910
with the Messages table
to get things like,

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:53.410
what was the subject
of that email

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:57.832
or like these other properties
that, if you were doing a more

00:24:57.832 --> 00:24:59.290
traditional
distributed system, you

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:02.020
might have to denormalize
and waste a lot of space

00:25:02.020 --> 00:25:03.802
to sort of get performance.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:10.900
And so, we use
interleaved rows a bunch.

00:25:13.630 --> 00:25:15.520
One other interesting thing--

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:22.180
you'll see these sort of weird
Is BCC or Is CC Boolean field

00:25:22.180 --> 00:25:23.910
at the bottom.

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:26.390
And so that's
basically saying, hey,

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:29.590
is this particular email
address in the BCC line?

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:30.820
Is it in the CC line?

00:25:30.820 --> 00:25:33.140
Like how are they
connected to this message?

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:35.810
And the interesting
thing there--

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:38.740
those rows actually
have two values,

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:40.480
which you might
expect from a Boolean.

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:43.870
But those values are
actually true or null.

00:25:43.870 --> 00:25:46.270
And so like, why
are we doing this?

00:25:46.270 --> 00:25:48.430
So Spanner actually
has this ability

00:25:48.430 --> 00:25:51.050
to have what are called
Null Filtered indexes.

00:25:51.050 --> 00:25:52.810
So if you're coming
from like PostgreSQL,

00:25:52.810 --> 00:25:55.540
or I'm sure Microsoft SQL
Server has a similar thing,

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:59.290
you might be familiar with this
concept of partial indexes.

00:25:59.290 --> 00:26:02.650
And that means that we can
create an index that's just--

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:08.320
is this person on the From line
or are they on the To line?

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:11.200
And it doesn't actually use
up any space for the Message

00:26:11.200 --> 00:26:15.490
Mailbox rows that don't have
the corresponding Boolean set

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:16.960
to true rather than null.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:21.640
It filters out all the
null entries, so yeah.

00:26:21.640 --> 00:26:23.440
So lessons learned
with Cloud Spanner.

00:26:23.440 --> 00:26:25.190
Setting up your schema
is super important,

00:26:25.190 --> 00:26:27.940
particularly if you're
putting a lot of data into it.

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:30.040
You know, it's pretty
easy to build and remove

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.320
secondary indexes,
but you are stuck

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:36.370
with your primary
key index, like you

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.750
have to rewrite rows if you're
changing your primary key.

00:26:38.750 --> 00:26:41.050
It's not magic like that.

00:26:41.050 --> 00:26:43.540
And it's pretty important
to just sort of play around

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:48.040
with the schema
to figure it out.

00:26:51.490 --> 00:26:52.990
So what challenges
have we run into?

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:55.360
So far I've been
like singing praises.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:58.300
And yeah, you know, no
plan survives contact

00:26:58.300 --> 00:27:01.810
with the enemy or the
database query planner.

00:27:01.810 --> 00:27:06.010
So one challenge we
ran into is actually

00:27:06.010 --> 00:27:08.800
Spanner is too smart
for its own good--

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:10.050
for our use case.

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:12.910
I guess your use case may vary.

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:18.610
So Spanner has a lot
of things or smarts

00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:22.390
in the query planner
to balance running

00:27:22.390 --> 00:27:24.260
a lot of small
transactional queries.

00:27:24.260 --> 00:27:25.900
So this is like the
kind of thing that

00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:28.630
is probably the 90% use case.

00:27:28.630 --> 00:27:33.130
It does things like if you
run a really big query,

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:36.190
it assumes that this must
be like an analytical query

00:27:36.190 --> 00:27:40.210
or maybe like a
backend bulk task.

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:43.420
And it will
deprioritize that query

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:46.090
and make sure that that query
takes a long time to run,

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:48.790
so it doesn't get in the way
of your transactional queries.

00:27:48.790 --> 00:27:54.130
But going back to our use
case, as we mentioned,

00:27:54.130 --> 00:27:56.830
you've all been on these
horrible, huge Reply All

00:27:56.830 --> 00:27:58.120
threads.

00:27:58.120 --> 00:28:01.690
And a lot of times, you know,
fortunately or unfortunately,

00:28:01.690 --> 00:28:06.190
those threads are the most
important, because they're

00:28:06.190 --> 00:28:09.550
the ones where the collaboration
story really gets tricky,

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.980
and you need to be able to share
some stuff but not other stuff.

00:28:12.980 --> 00:28:16.990
And we really need
those to be answerable

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:22.210
in a short enough amount
of time that it doesn't

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:24.970
degrade the Gmail experience.

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:27.580
And so what we were
running into was

00:28:27.580 --> 00:28:29.410
when we did the
queries to span out--

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.350
to fan out on those
really big threads,

00:28:32.350 --> 00:28:35.110
it was not overwhelming
anything else,

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:36.550
but it was taking a long time.

00:28:36.550 --> 00:28:40.000
And there wasn't really a great
way to tell the query planner,

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:43.720
hey, this query is really
important, go ahead and do it.

00:28:43.720 --> 00:28:47.120
And we tried some experiments
with fanning out and sending

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:48.520
a bunch of queries.

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:51.100
We tried some
experiments with doing

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:54.760
like a little bit of caching
in like Redis or something

00:28:54.760 --> 00:28:58.330
like that, using
Cloud Memorystore,

00:28:58.330 --> 00:29:01.300
but we just didn't find a thing
that really delivered the user

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:04.730
experience that we wanted.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:06.867
So that led us to
our next database

00:29:06.867 --> 00:29:08.200
that we're going to chat about--

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:10.930
Cloud Bigtable.

00:29:10.930 --> 00:29:13.780
So Cloud Bigtable
is actually based

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:18.550
on Bigtable, which is
now, at this point, a very

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:22.000
well-established
Google technology.

00:29:22.000 --> 00:29:25.840
It sort of has a lot
less functionality

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:28.450
than Clouds Spanner,
Cloud Datastore.

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.430
It's really just
a key value store

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:32.995
or a sorted key-value
store, let's say.

00:29:32.995 --> 00:29:34.870
And basically, that
means that there's really

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:37.070
two queries you can do to it.

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:40.280
You can query for this
particular primary key--

00:29:40.280 --> 00:29:41.590
give me the value.

00:29:41.590 --> 00:29:45.850
Or you can do lexicographical--
however you pronounce that

00:29:45.850 --> 00:29:46.450
word--

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:50.900
range queries and
basically say, hey,

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:57.070
give me all values for keys
between A and BE or AA and B.

00:29:57.070 --> 00:30:00.310
And so there, it's like a
pretty limited query interface,

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:05.190
but the trade-off you get here
is that it's really, really

00:30:05.190 --> 00:30:08.050
simple and really, really fast.

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.960
And so there,
basically each node,

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:11.180
you add to Cloud Bigtable.

00:30:11.180 --> 00:30:12.555
And it's another
manage database,

00:30:12.555 --> 00:30:16.170
so you can just add and remove
nodes to your heart's desire,

00:30:16.170 --> 00:30:19.350
and you pay for more nodes,
get more performance.

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:20.790
When it's nighttime,
you can just

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.220
like decrease the
number of nodes

00:30:23.220 --> 00:30:26.340
and not pay for what
you're not using.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:29.440
But basically, each
node you add gives you

00:30:29.440 --> 00:30:33.000
10,000 reads or writes a
second, which is a lot of reads

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:34.740
or writes a second.

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:36.930
And basically, the
queries just look

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:39.720
like either put some
data in for this key,

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:44.130
or read some data for
this key or set of keys.

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:45.910
There's currently two modes.

00:30:45.910 --> 00:30:48.910
The mode that we use for this--

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:53.408
just because for our
use case, it's not great

00:30:53.408 --> 00:30:55.200
if the feature is
unavailable, but it's not

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:56.790
like business ending.

00:30:56.790 --> 00:30:59.700
People can still get
at their Streak box

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:03.660
data, which is stored
safely in Cloud Datastore.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:08.130
But there's one mode that is
single-zone availability--

00:31:08.130 --> 00:31:11.810
has the concept of a transaction
within a particular row.

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:14.880
And then there's another mode
that is multi-zone replication.

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:16.290
You forgo transactions.

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:19.710
But even if one zone goes
down-- which is infrequent,

00:31:19.710 --> 00:31:21.060
but happens--

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:24.900
you can just access the
data in another zone,

00:31:24.900 --> 00:31:26.820
and then it'll
asynchronously replicate

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:29.980
after the zone comes back up.

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:30.480
So yeah.

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:34.260
So Cloud Bigtable-- not a
great tool for every job

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:35.648
but the best tool for some jobs.

00:31:35.648 --> 00:31:37.440
And we're actually
using this in production

00:31:37.440 --> 00:31:42.300
for querying this
email metadata index.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:45.580
And let me show you, sort
of, what that looks like.

00:31:45.580 --> 00:31:49.590
So here, we've got
the output of CBT--

00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:54.100
is the Cloud Bigtable console.

00:31:54.100 --> 00:31:58.470
And this is our Messages table.

00:31:58.470 --> 00:32:00.180
And you can see,
there's this concept

00:32:00.180 --> 00:32:06.480
of a column family, which
is sort of a set of data

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.670
that's stored together.

00:32:08.670 --> 00:32:12.390
And we have one column family
for each of those indexes

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:15.050
that we need to
traverse the graph.

00:32:15.050 --> 00:32:18.180
And so there, for instance,
I was showing you the Message

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:22.080
Mailboxes table, and so we've
got one column family that's

00:32:22.080 --> 00:32:24.690
sort of this index
for looking up

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:29.260
Message Mailboxes by
the domain or by email.

00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:31.170
And then also,
messages are indexed

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.990
by the message ID, the RFC
message ID, or the thread

00:32:33.990 --> 00:32:37.200
ID, which lets us do that
sort of graph traversal.

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:40.890
And the cool thing here
is the Bigtable API

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:42.270
is completely asynchronous.

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:45.000
So we basically, when we're
doing this graph query,

00:32:45.000 --> 00:32:47.700
we can just have a queue
that's just as soon

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:49.620
as information comes
back, sending out

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.300
the next set of fetches.

00:32:51.300 --> 00:32:55.290
And that's all pipelined and can
be very much like sub-second.

00:32:55.290 --> 00:32:59.050
So each look up is
about 6 milliseconds,

00:32:59.050 --> 00:33:01.440
so you can do a lot of
lookups before somebody

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.530
notices the delay.

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:06.100
Other things here.

00:33:06.100 --> 00:33:08.380
This is very much sort
of build-it-yourself.

00:33:08.380 --> 00:33:13.320
So the model in Bigtable
is just a string

00:33:13.320 --> 00:33:16.500
of bytes to a string of bytes.

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:20.070
And so, we had to figure
out our own encoding that

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:23.800
does graphic encoding
the way that we want.

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:25.290
There's a few
libraries out there.

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:28.800
There's one called Orderly.

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:31.170
There's one that's sort
of partially factored out

00:33:31.170 --> 00:33:33.717
of the HBase library.

00:33:33.717 --> 00:33:35.550
So there's some tooling
there, but you still

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:38.010
need to very much figure
out your own schema

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:39.810
and your own encoding.

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:43.170
This was a bit more
developer-intensive,

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:45.000
so definitely don't
recommend reaching

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:47.460
for this from the get-go.

00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:49.470
But it is very, very
fast, and it really

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:50.910
does what it says on the tin.

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.550
So, you know, we're using Cloud
Bigtable for those lookups

00:33:53.550 --> 00:33:54.640
in production.

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:55.380
6 milliseconds.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:58.750
You can do a whole bunch
of reads all at once,

00:33:58.750 --> 00:34:01.560
and it's like
providing what we need.

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:03.660
And so then, at
the end of the day,

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:06.670
just wanted to summarize sort
of what we already talked about

00:34:06.670 --> 00:34:09.590
in a convenient flowchart.

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:11.310
And so kind of
the first question

00:34:11.310 --> 00:34:13.620
I'd ask if I were reaching
for a new database

00:34:13.620 --> 00:34:19.710
is how developed is your
project schema product,

00:34:19.710 --> 00:34:22.139
however you want
to think about it.

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:26.340
If you're still in that sort
of finding product market fit

00:34:26.340 --> 00:34:29.610
or experimenting
with the project,

00:34:29.610 --> 00:34:32.280
then I think either Cloud
Datastore or Cloud Firestore is

00:34:32.280 --> 00:34:34.570
probably where you want to go.

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:38.219
Basically, there
are key things here.

00:34:38.219 --> 00:34:40.230
It's got very flexible schemas.

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:43.110
You just like set
up the documents,

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.360
and then you can index a
bunch of different ways

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:47.110
after the fact.

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:48.840
It's very easy to work with.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:52.587
If you're using Cloud
Firestore for a new project,

00:34:52.587 --> 00:34:54.420
you can actually use
that in Firestore mode,

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:57.798
get most of the same thing--
oh, sorry, in real-time mode,

00:34:57.798 --> 00:35:00.090
get most of the same benefits
that we're talking about,

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:04.650
and get being able to send stuff
directly to your mobile or web

00:35:04.650 --> 00:35:07.150
clients.

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:10.533
So I think that if you're
still figuring that out,

00:35:10.533 --> 00:35:11.450
that's a great choice.

00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:14.520
Oh, also, Firestore
and Cloud Datastore

00:35:14.520 --> 00:35:17.230
just have a per-operation
building model,

00:35:17.230 --> 00:35:18.935
so if you're not
using it, you're

00:35:18.935 --> 00:35:21.060
only paying for storage,
which is like pretty cheap

00:35:21.060 --> 00:35:23.040
if you're just getting started.

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:26.490
Whereas, Cloud Bigtable or Cloud
Spanner are a per-node pricing

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:28.800
model, so you're paying
hourly for each node

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:30.193
that you have set up.

00:35:30.193 --> 00:35:31.860
So if you're just
sort of experimenting,

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:33.777
it's real cheap to get
started with like Cloud

00:35:33.777 --> 00:35:36.233
Firestore or Cloud Datastore.

00:35:36.233 --> 00:35:38.400
And then, yeah, on the
difference between those two,

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:41.980
I sort of jokingly put,
is it 2019 or later?

00:35:41.980 --> 00:35:44.190
But yeah really, Cloud
Firestore Datastore mode

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:46.380
is just Cloud
Datastore but better.

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:48.630
They've announced that they're
going to move everybody

00:35:48.630 --> 00:35:50.310
to Cloud Firestore.

00:35:50.310 --> 00:35:51.810
So if you're starting
a new project,

00:35:51.810 --> 00:35:55.140
just start with Cloud Firestore.

00:35:55.140 --> 00:35:59.580
I have trouble envisioning
a case in which you actively

00:35:59.580 --> 00:36:02.025
want to go to Datastore if
you're starting a new project.

00:36:05.130 --> 00:36:07.730
So if you're more certain of
the needs for your project,

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:10.620
if this is something
where either it's

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:14.190
a new technology for an
established company or product,

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:19.560
or if you're really, really
certain of your performance

00:36:19.560 --> 00:36:23.370
needs and you've tested it out
and have a really good idea--

00:36:23.370 --> 00:36:25.620
then that's when you should
probably reach for a Cloud

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:27.590
Bigtable or Cloud Spanner.

00:36:27.590 --> 00:36:30.000
Cloud Spanner is a lot
nicer to work with.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.920
It has data types,
it has SQL, it

00:36:31.920 --> 00:36:34.920
has these globally
consistent transactions.

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:38.890
So generally, unless you know
you're going to need to really,

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:41.380
really optimize those
key value lookups

00:36:41.380 --> 00:36:46.235
and build things, like this
horrible Reply All thread case,

00:36:46.235 --> 00:36:47.610
definitely go with
Cloud Spanner.

00:36:47.610 --> 00:36:49.910
We actually built out this
index entirely on Cloud--

00:36:49.910 --> 00:36:54.330
or this metadata indexing
system entirely on Cloud Spanner

00:36:54.330 --> 00:36:55.260
to start with.

00:36:55.260 --> 00:36:57.600
I'm super happy that we
did, even though we ended up

00:36:57.600 --> 00:37:00.300
moving some of the
workload to Cloud Bigtable

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:03.000
because it informed a
lot of our decisions,

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:06.185
sort of gave us more
information on the use case.

00:37:06.185 --> 00:37:07.560
And then if you
just like really,

00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:11.160
really need that one specific
tool, then Cloud Bigtable

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:13.410
is probably what you
should reach for.

00:37:13.410 --> 00:37:17.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

